WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:05.640
Before we explain the motion-captured mouse
who I’ve named Moby Fitzsimmons,

00:00:07.318 --> 00:00:08.673
Let’s go to basics.

00:00:08.673 --> 00:00:13.920
We’ve all seen some photo like this one, with&nbsp;
some poor actor in a weird outfit, covered in dots

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.153
whether it’s on “The Hulk”&nbsp;
Mark Ruffalo’s instagram

00:00:17.153 --> 00:00:21.595
or Star Wars’ C3PO’s Anthony&nbsp;
Daniels on his account.
 &nbsp;

00:00:21.595 --> 00:00:23.757
But where does that motion…go?

00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:27.534
By focusing less on the actors&nbsp;
and more on how it works

00:00:27.534 --> 00:00:30.960
you can understand the real&nbsp;artistry
behind motion capture.

00:00:33.418 --> 00:00:35.797
There are a few main ways to capture motion.&nbsp;

00:00:35.797 --> 00:00:40.274
This is Rokoko’s Smartsuit Pro&nbsp;
and it uses inertial sensors.

00:00:40.274 --> 00:00:44.155
These broadcast the location of&nbsp;
devices embedded inside the suit

00:00:44.155 --> 00:00:47.530
kinda like how your phone&nbsp;
knows which way it’s turned.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:53.555
More common in high end video games and movies,
you’ll see optical tracking of markers

00:00:53.555 --> 00:00:57.600
in which a camera is learning where parts of
a person’s body are by looking

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.000
for high contrast areas.

00:01:00.918 --> 00:01:03.402
Jimmy Corvan directed business development for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.402 --> 00:01:07.072
motion capture studio, House&nbsp;of Moves
 for about ten years.

00:01:07.072 --> 00:01:09.540
It worked on everything from the Injustice series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.900
to Mortal Kombat to Marvel&nbsp;
movies to Barbie's blog.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:18.577
"Comment, like, and subscribe so you can get&nbsp;
the latest and greatest Barbie vlogs and more!"

00:01:18.577 --> 00:01:23.196
Is it more common in high end&nbsp;
stuff like what y'all are doing?

00:01:23.196 --> 00:01:27.498
Is that pretty much always going to be&nbsp;
optical thing, rather than a sensor?

00:01:27.498 --> 00:01:29.520
For the foreseeable future

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.620
and I'm sure there's somebody at one of the
inertial companies that will correct me on this

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:38.875
but in all of our testing and&nbsp;
everything that we've found...

00:01:39.919 --> 00:01:42.720
optical motion capture is sub-millimeter accurate.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.678
These suits don’t have any sensors at all.

00:01:47.678 --> 00:01:51.360
They’re basically fashion,&nbsp;
made to be seen really clearly.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.560
I will always get the question: How hard are they?

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.300
How do you get the technology in the balls?

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.189
And I'm like, well, you can squeeze them.

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:03.360
They're just retro reflective tape on&nbsp;
the outside of little squishy balls.

00:02:05.446 --> 00:02:09.180
The footage shot with the suits on is&nbsp;
fed through software that interprets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.695
what the camera sees, before artists review it.

00:02:12.695 --> 00:02:15.657
If you look at Mark Ruffalo’s&nbsp;
tweet of him, Tom Holland,&nbsp;

00:02:15.657 --> 00:02:17.880
and Don Cheadle on Avenger’s Endgame,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.120
you can see how additional symbols allow software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.156
to know more about the location&nbsp;
of the suit on the performer.

00:02:25.156 --> 00:02:28.860
Even if one symbol is dark&nbsp;
or out of focus, the software&nbsp;

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.620
can figure out what’s going&nbsp;
on thanks to the pattern.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.446
The first step in the in the cleanup&nbsp;
process is somebody goes through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.446 --> 00:02:40.853
and makes sure that you can see every one of&nbsp;
those dots in every frame of the shot.

00:02:40.853 --> 00:02:43.627
It sounds tedious because it is.

00:02:43.627 --> 00:02:45.978
So all the markers have a different name

00:02:45.978 --> 00:02:51.840
and somebody's job is going through making sure
you can see each marker in every single frame

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.848
and that each marker is properly named.

00:02:54.848 --> 00:02:57.847
That process is called tracking and labeling.

00:02:57.847 --> 00:03:00.300
This can be augmented with&nbsp;
facial capture like this

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.397
a range of motion capture gloves

00:03:02.397 --> 00:03:06.755
and even the ability to label&nbsp;
and motion track objects.

00:03:06.755 --> 00:03:09.894
For hobbyists, there’s&nbsp;
stuff like Moby Fitzsimmons.

00:03:09.894 --> 00:03:13.634
His form comes from a free to&nbsp;
download mocap-ready website.

00:03:13.634 --> 00:03:18.697
I captured his movements using AI called Plask
that just figured out the motion

00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:20.994
without any dots or sensors.

00:03:20.994 --> 00:03:26.700
These methods can vary wildly in cost and quality
but the output is actually similar.

00:03:28.915 --> 00:03:30.082
It’s bones.

00:03:32.682 --> 00:03:35.776
This structure is the basic&nbsp;
output of most motion captures

00:03:35.776 --> 00:03:38.056
no cloth, no muscle, no hair

00:03:38.056 --> 00:03:41.671
just what the software calculates as the skeleton.

00:03:41.671 --> 00:03:45.832
Each dot here is a joint, while&nbsp;
the longer shapes are bones.

00:03:45.832 --> 00:03:49.800
In this example, they’re... my bones.
Soon to be Moby’s.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.440
These bones are given a hierarchy

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:57.013
so if the top of your arm rotates,
the lower part usually will too.

00:03:57.013 --> 00:04:00.300
These subtle variations can&nbsp;
convey a lot of movement.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.334
These numbers represent the&nbsp;
rotation of a single elbow joint.

00:04:05.334 --> 00:04:08.980
Watch how they change as the&nbsp;
elbow moves during the animation.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:13.440
We are trying to figure out&nbsp;
exactly where the skeleton exists

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.900
because that's what we're really capturing,
is your skeleton moving.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.000
We aren't capturing the flesh on top.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.297
We've got very, very close, but it is not perfect.

00:04:21.297 --> 00:04:25.320
If the bone lengths are just slightly shorter
or just slightly longer

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.720
then even something as simple as clapping

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.940
like if I were to do this, it might end up
that they go through each other like this

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.814
because the bone lengths are different.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.680
That's where animators come in.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.935
There's a lot of that fixing and&nbsp;
you end up seeing it in postures.

00:04:41.935 --> 00:04:46.140
There's a very popular phrase in&nbsp;
the mocap space called monkey butt.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:49.835
And that is the the hips&nbsp;
kind of jut back a little bit&nbsp;

00:04:49.835 --> 00:04:52.200
and it kind of looks like they have a monkey butt.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.960
And so an animator will need&nbsp;
to go through and push the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.360
hips back forward, kind of undo that work.

00:04:58.793 --> 00:05:04.440
This outfit provides a clue to why motion capture
is harder than it looks after the data is cleaned.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.613
The Hulk is significantly&nbsp;
larger than Mark Ruffalo.

00:05:08.613 --> 00:05:12.300
VFX artists take the time to&nbsp;
make Taika Waititi’s capture&nbsp;

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.840
fit the body of Korg, but&nbsp;
you can see what goes wrong

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.456
without extra work.

00:05:17.456 --> 00:05:20.678
See how Moby’s hands run into his face here?

00:05:20.678 --> 00:05:24.420
That’s not a problem when I do it
because I don’t have a head like Moby’s.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.358
The same goes for position in space.

00:05:26.358 --> 00:05:28.472
See how his feet wobble around?

00:05:28.472 --> 00:05:30.420
This is part of why you don’t see tracking images

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:34.536
on the actor modeling for Groot,
a tall talking tree

00:05:34.536 --> 00:05:38.400
or actor Sean Gunn, who stands in
for Rocket Raccoon.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:44.280
These figures, and even motion captured figures,
are too difficult to graft on human motion.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.253
So the people just used for reference.

00:05:47.253 --> 00:05:50.700
Even if you're not using all&nbsp;
of the data, specifically

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.474
having the timing created&nbsp;
by the actor or performer&nbsp;

00:05:55.474 --> 00:05:57.251
is really, really helpful.

00:05:57.251 --> 00:05:59.160
A lot of people, like myself included

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:04.020
I talk with my hands, and if an animator tried
to animate me talking with my hands

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.495
I'd get the timing wrong.

00:06:05.495 --> 00:06:08.580
Look at this side by side&nbsp;
of Hulk and Mark Ruffalo.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:11.658
Ruffalo gives a great performance&nbsp;
that you can really see.

00:06:11.658 --> 00:06:16.440
But look at Hulk’s shoulder size, and the cloth
and the space his hands take up.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:23.520
You can even see, in this clip, how his right hand
has been tweaked completely after capture.

00:06:29.085 --> 00:06:33.039
The way that motion capture&nbsp;
tends to get covered is...

00:06:33.374 --> 00:06:36.098
look Andy Serkis and look at Gollum.

00:06:36.098 --> 00:06:42.316
They do a wonderful job of performing,&nbsp;
but there is a huge gap in between.

00:06:42.316 --> 00:06:45.950
That is months and months and&nbsp;
months and months of work.

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:48.300
It's portrayed is just like&nbsp;
there is an animate button.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.433
Look at all the changes within this short clip.

00:06:51.433 --> 00:06:55.113
That level of detail would take a long time
for people to animate.

00:06:55.113 --> 00:06:57.600
But the limited structure of the final format&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.780
means there’s a ton of&nbsp;
animation after the capture.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.940
Mocap depends on the quality of the capture
and the work put into it.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.016
That’s what makes animation feel real.

00:07:08.016 --> 00:07:11.400
Without that, it’s just joints and bones.

00:07:15.278 --> 00:07:22.020
Space Jam 2, legitimately&nbsp;
we set up a system around&nbsp;

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:25.020
a professional sized basketball court.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.394
The court that is in the movie&nbsp;
is the court that we shot on

00:07:29.394 --> 00:07:32.520
and they wrapped the entire thing in
a giant green screen.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:33.855
The whole thing.

00:07:33.855 --> 00:07:41.340
So you have this self-contained volume that is
holding all the air in itself

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:45.356
because it's just all the way to the ceiling,
green screened all the way to the floor

00:07:45.356 --> 00:07:47.134
360 degrees around.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:55.644
And the director wanted to, instead of
adding in fog effects in post

00:07:55.644 --> 00:07:59.349
he pumped a bunch of fog into this volume

00:07:59.349 --> 00:08:03.840
and I don't expect people&nbsp;
to know what that does to&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.496
optical motion capture

00:08:05.496 --> 00:08:09.540
but to say you can see fog and our cameras
need to be able to see.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:17.160
The way they work is the camera shoots light from
a strobe around the camera at those little markers

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:20.589
and that little markers bounces the light
right back to the camera

00:08:20.589 --> 00:08:23.837
and fog introduces a bunch of little
water molecules into the air

00:08:23.837 --> 00:08:27.053
and water molecules scatter light.

00:08:27.053 --> 00:08:33.056
So, we are now shooting in a&nbsp;
volume that is filled with water&nbsp;

00:08:33.056 --> 00:08:35.535
and just bouncing light all over the place.

